ed strict rules of  its own upheld by its supreme court  i think the newfoundland judgment would  give provinces  quite a bit of comfort that a  carefully crafted set of restrictions  that was rooted in science  and that was backed up by the best  empirical evidence  and that had a sufficiently broad set  of exemptions would likely survive  constitutional scrutiny so i think  provinces would have to work a bit  harder to justify  a measure like this the canadian civil  liberties association is appealing the  newfoundland and labrador  case and important to remember all  provinces have unique  challenges due to geography and size  with some borders like bcs  making it more difficult to seal than  the atlantic  abigail beeman global news ottawa  a coven 19 outbreak at one of canada  post's busiest processing  plants is getting bigger 149 employees  at the mississauga facility have now  tested positive for the virus  in a statement canada post says public  health has confirmed  that it is able to maintain its  operations any employees who do test  positive  will leave the workplace and  self-isolate more than 4 500 people  work at the site the biden  administration enters office a year into  the u.s  pandemic the president has promised to  provide a hundred million covet 19  vaccinations in his first  100 days in office but is there enough  vaccine to accomplish that  as mike armstrong reports at least a  dozen states are reporting shortages  the road to recovery is turning out to  be slower than predicted  states that were ready with mass  vaccination programs  are only limitations just can't run them  our  only limitation as we stand here today  is supply from the federal government  the former trump administration pushed  states to expand vaccination programs to  get shots into arms  the problem has been getting the  vaccines to the states  california ohio and west virginia are  among states warning they're running out  florida is rolling back who's eligible  and new york is canceling appointments  for next week as it braces for new  strains  the new strains are frightening the uk  strain brazil strain now the south  africa strain and they're going to be  more strains  i would wager on it new jersey's  governor toured a new vaccination mega  site friday but he's had strong  criticism  of the trump administration on supply  force he says  over promising and under delivery  operation warp speed  set goals that weren't reached 20  million people were supposed to be  vaccinated by the end of december  as of thursday the cdc says only 15  million people have been vaccinated  of that less than three million have  gotten the full two doses  the first one is the economy the biden  administration says it's still getting  its sea legs and will tackle supply  but friday the president signed two  executive orders aimed at  economic relief for families small  businesses  and struggling communities police  officers firefighters first responders  nurses they're all at the risk of losing  their jobs  now even as the country struggles to  reach its goals there are calls to move  them  even higher as new strains spread it  could drive up  infection rates if more of the public  isn't vaccinated  mike armstrong global news washington  the u.s senate has approved biden's  nomination for pentagon  chief retired army general lloyd austin  is the first  black defense secretary in u.s history  biden is looking to restore stability at  the pentagon which went through six  people in that position some permanent  some temporary during the trump  administration  sleepless in small town alberta coming  up the concerns about  coal mining leaving some people anxious  the federal government is suspending the  sale of all decommissioned rcmp vehicles  the announcement comes nine months after  a gunman disguised as an rcmp officer  used an old police vehicle during a  deadly shooting rampage  in nova scotia 22 people were killed in  one of the worst massacres in canadian  history  earlier this month a 23-year-old from  nova scotia was arrested for  impersonating a police officer  while driving a former police vehicle  outfitted with lights  and decals alberta needs to diversify  given the cancellation of the keystone  xl pipeline  so the province is turning to new  investment in metallurgic coal mining  the kind used to make steel there are  rich deposits of this coal in the  southwest part of the province  an australian mining company could get  its grassy mountain project  in the crow's nest pass greenlit by the  end of this year  but it's the government's move to  quietly open the region to further  exploration last spring  that is sparking outrage heather yorks  west reports  conservationist kevin bantigam has a  little cabin in southwest alberta  it sits near the banks of the old man  river an incredibly  important source of water in a region  that's pretty dry  virtually all our water comes from the  headwaters  from the eastern slopes of the rocky  mountains the areas that  trap a lot of winter snow and get a lot  of spring rain it's why vantigam is so  concerned about the potential resurgence  of coal mining here we're directly  impacted it's in our grazing  range ranchers laura lange and john  smith say the issue has been keeping  them up at night  all i can think about is you know the  blood sweat and tears that goes into  these places  and to have it undone  and under threat is is  it's hard to fathom across the border in  bc's elk valley coal mining has been a  major industry for decades  but in the crow's nest pass there hasn't  been an active coal operation  since the 1960s several years ago an  australian mining company applied to  reopen the abandoned grassy mountain  mine  the project is now before a joint review  panel but it has support from the local  town council  it's definitely an act would be an  economic boost to our community  and and allow us to be sustainable  moving forward in the future what's  important to understand is this is one  of the first mines  of its size in in canada that's been  designed from the ground up  to to manage issues like like water  quality  rivers dale resources says the project  will use new technology to minimize the  mine's impact on the environment but the  project isn't the only source of concern  in may alberta's government rescinded a  decades-old coal policy  that had banned strip mining in  sensitive areas of the rockies  since then dozens of leases have been  handed out and at some sites  exploration is well underway they've  created a big disturbance there we've  got all sorts of eroding surfaces and  all sorts of  damage done to that mountain and there  isn't even a mining proposal yet all  they're doing is exploring it  but in the last few weeks the public  outcry has grown country music singers  like paul brant and core blund  took to social media to show their  concern several petitions have now  attracted more than 100  000 signatures and last week a group  opposed to the coal policy changes even  took the province to court  a decision is expected in the next  couple of months  heather urex west global news calgary  still ahead a review of ontario's  vaccine rollout  and where it fell short  when compared to other countries around  the world canada's kova 19 vaccination  campaign is falling behind  in december canada ranked fifth that's  when the roll-up began  but new data shows this country has  dropped to 15th  as of yesterday canada had vaccinated a  total of more than  738 000 people  ontario is preparing to launch up to 60  vaccination sites  as part of the second phase of its  campaign this spring retired general  rick hillier who leads the province's  vaccine task force told global news that  ontario will be teaming up with  pharmacies and family physicians  tonight carolyn jarvis looks at the  first phase the missteps  successes and lessons learned  every time the phone rings kristin  hunter has a pang of anxiety  that it's her father's long-term care  home calling to say his health  has taken a turn he got infected with  covet 19 unfortunately on january the  8th  and i see it as preventable given that  though that vaccine was possible to be  in his arm before he got infected while  ontario has prioritized covet 19  vaccines for long-term care residents  and employees  alongside health care workers and first  nation communities  it has fallen behind other provinces in  reaching those seniors  quickly and we only have 626 homes  we have 72 000 people we know where they  are and we've had enough vaccine to  vaccinate all of them  from december 21st one of ontario's most  critical early decisions was to place  its vaccination hubs  in 19 hospitals but with instructions  from pfizer at the outset not to move  the vaccine  most long-term care residents couldn't  reach it  there was credible concern  about moving the vaccine that if you  move it around and actually if you  defrosted it and agitated and shook it  up it would denature it  quebec by contrast elected to put half  its vaccination centers  right inside long-term care homes and by  mid-january with fewer vaccines supplied  it has given first shots to more  long-term care residents  than ontario once pfizer provided  new distribution guidelines for its  vaccine on december 18th  british columbia started moving the  vaccine into long-term care homes  days later but ontario didn't do so  until january 5th so yeah there's clear  bumps on the road there were clear  hurdles and some missteps by then  ontario's new approach was called  speed over perfection empty the freezers  and get shots into arms but speed  meant some back office staff in  hospitals were offered the shot  before frontline workers so speed over  precision is not what we were hoping to  see  and it's certainly not fair in its  latest pivot ontario is saying long-term  care homes will be its near sole focus  only for some it comes too late carolyn  jarvis global news  toronto and to learn more about  ontario's vaccine roll out its successes  and missteps to date watch the new  reality tomorrow night at seven with  donna friesen  right here on global up next remembering  the achievements of hammering hank aaron  on and off the field  the world of baseball is looking back at  the life of a true champion tonight  hank aaron a hall of famer chased down  and broke babe ruth's home run record  but it's not just how he played the game  it's how aaron stood up to racism  as mike delay reports he was a humble  hero who fought to change america  strikes aaron waiting the outfield deep  and straight away  even after his greatest professional  moment hank aaron had every reason in  the world to be angry  he'd just broken one of baseball's  hallowed records held  by an icon in babe ruth yet for many  race is what still defined him a black  man is getting a standing ovation in the  deep south  for breaking a record of an all-time  baseball idol  in the weeks leading up to that moment  he was getting 3 000 letters a day  much of it full of unvarnished hate he  read them all  forty years later when he said he didn't  think america had changed he read all  the new ones that came in too  henry aaron grew up in mobile alabama  when he was a boy the ku klux klan came  to his family home  his mother told him to hide under the  bed he looked at baseball as his way out  and while he became a star he never  achieved the same fame or enjoy the same  rewards  as white stars like joe dimaggio or  mickey mantle  but his impact was profound it made him  mentally tough it gave him an  understanding that he wasn't just  playing for henry aaron  he was literally carrying an entire race  of people on his shoulders  body break star hal johnson says he  didn't believe his father  when he said he played with aaron in the  negro leagues at a game in montreal  johnson's father said he was going to  say hi  hal a bit shy back then refused to go  hank came out of the dugout looked at my  dad  and and opened his arms my dad goes on  the field  and my sister and big hug and by the  time i got down he was  he would stop the field aaron was his  idol as he was for so many others  including canadian hall of famer  ferguson jenkins and musician lenny  kravitz  who proudly wore a hank aaron t-shirt as  a kid  hank aaron you know he was he was just  he was just a great man  regardless of his color and he wasn't a  great black man he was just a great man  for what he did hammer and hank aaron  was 86.  what a nice story and that is global  national for this friday i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is a herd of deer  taking a stroll through prince george bc  we would love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your  photos  to viewers at globalnational.com thank  you for watching and i'll see you back  here tomorrow  have a great night  [Music]  you

